Towards Deploying VLA without Fine-Tuning: Plug-and-Play Inference-Time VLA Policy Steering via Embodied Evolutionary Diffusion (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.14178v1)
citeturn6529view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({"ref_id":"turn6523view4","lineno":94}); Total lines: 275
L86: where $\{a_{t}^{i}\}_{i=1}^{M}\sim\pi_{\text{vla}}(a_{t}\mid c_{t})$ denotes $M$ i.i.d. action proposals sampled from the pre-trained VLA policy, and $R(a_{t};c_{t})$ is a steering objective reward that measures how well action $a_{t}$ aligns with the task context $c_{t}$. Solving the policy steering problem defined in Eq. cite42†1 requires two key capabilities: steering objective reasoning and action proposal optimization.
L87: The first involves inferring a task-aligned objective reward $R(a_{t};c_{t})$ from the context $c_{t}$. VLA-Pilot addresses this via the EPS-CoT module, which leverages MLLMs to reason about open-world steering objectives. The second capability involves searching within the action distribution of the pre-trained VLA policy to identify feasible actions.
L88: VLA-Pilot addresses this via Evolutionary Diffusion, which iteratively scores and mutates sampled action candidates based on the steering reward $R(a_{t};c_{t})$, enabling effective adaptation to downstream task requirements.
L89: ### III-B Steering Objective Reasoning
L90: The first step in policy steering is to infer a task-aligned steering objective reward $R(a_{t};c_{t})$ from the given context $c_{t}$. Our key insight is that the policy steering problem in robotic foundation models bears a strong analogy to the prompting problem in large language models (LLMs).
L91: Therefore, inspired by the effectiveness of Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting [cite43†17 ] in LLMs, we propose Embodied Policy Steering Chain-of-Thought $\mathcal{F}_{\text{EPS-CoT}}$, a structured reasoning module designed to generate steering objective rewards:
L92:  | $$R(a_{t};c_{t})=\mathcal{F}_{\text{EPS-CoT}}(\Phi_{\text{MLLM}}(c_{t}))$$  |  | (2)
L93: EPS-CoT decomposes the reasoning process into four interleaved stages. It begins with steering goal confirmation, where the MLLM is prompted to rephrase and verify the language instruction in order to ensure alignment between the task demand and the steering goal. Following this, the MLLM performs scenario understanding, where it interprets the task context and identifies potential action modes based on the visual observation.
L94: This step facilitates a high-level understanding of the task scenario, including environmental affordances, spatial relationships, and task-relevant entities. To further ground embodied information in the reasoning process , EPS-CoT incorporates embodied augmentation [cite44†18 ], which enhances the reasoning by integrating spatial keypoints of robot end-effector position and object location extracted via vision foundation models, namely DINO [cite45†19 ] and SAM [cite46†20 ].
L95: Finally, based on scenario understanding and embodied information, EPS-CoT infers the task-aligned steering objective and generates the corresponding scoring reward code. Given the inherent ambiguity and imprecision of natural language instructions, we implement the reward as a non-differentiable black-box scoring function [cite47†21 ]. This formulation effectively captures the vague yet goal-directed nature of language, while also simplifying the reasoning process required by the MLLM.
L96: An overview of the EPS-CoT reasoning process is illustrated in Figure cite48†3 .
L97: cite49†Image: Refer to caption Fig. 3: Embodied Policy Steering Chain-of-Thought. EPS-CoT guides the steering objective reasoning process through a structured CoT.
L98: ### III-C Action Proposal Optimization
L99: 
L100: To improve task alignment of initial action proposals, we introduce an Evolutionary Diffusion algorithm that synergistically leverages the multimodal expressiveness of diffusion process and the black-box optimization of evolutionary search [cite47†21 , cite50†22 ]. The proposed algorithm begins by sampling initial action proposals $A_{0}$ of $M$ actions using the pre-trained VLA policy:
L101: 
L102:  | $$A^{0}=\{a_{t}^{i}\}_{i=1}^{M}\sim\pi_{\text{vla}}(a_{t}\mid c_{t}),$$  |  | (3)
L103: We then performs an evolutionary search loop to iteratively evaluate and mutate the initial proposal set using the steering objective reward $R(a_{t};c_{t})$. Specifically, at each evolutionary iteration $k$, we score the proposal set $\{R(a_{t}^{i};c_{t})|a_{t}^{i}\in A^{k}\}_{i=1}^{M}$ and select high-scoring elite proposals $E^{k+1}\subseteq A^{k}$:
L104: 
L105:  | $$q(a_{t})=\frac{\exp\left(\tau R(a_{t};c_{t})\right)}{\sum_{i=1}^{M}\exp\left(\tau R(a_{t}^{i};c_{t})\right)}$$  |  | (4)
L106:  | $$E^{k+1}=\left\{a_{t}^{i}\overset{\text{iid}}{\sim}q(a_{t})\right\}_{i=1}^{M},$$  |  | (5)
L107: 
L108: where $\tau$ is a tunable temperature parameter controlling the sharpness of $q$. To further enhance proposal diversity and explore task-aligned actions, we apply a truncated diffusion-denoising process to mutate elite proposals $E^{k+1}$ (see Figure cite51†4 ). Specifically, we run the first $n$ steps of the forward diffusion process to obtain noised elite proposals $\bar{E}^{k+1}$:
L109:  | $$\bar{E}^{k+1}=\left\{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{N}}a_{t}+\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{N}}\,\epsilon\,\middle|\,a_{t}\in E^{k+1}\right\},$$  |  | (6)
L110: where $\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)$. However, directly applying forward diffusion may lead to $\bar{E}^{k+1}$ drifting out of the original VLA distribution due to the stochastic nature of the noise. Therefore, we subsequently perform the final $n$ steps of the reverse diffusion process, using the noise predictor from the pre-trained VLA policy to denoise $\bar{E}^{k+1}$ and obtain the refined proposals $A^{k+1}$ for propagation, ensuring they lie within the original data manifold:
L111:  | $$A^{k+1}=\left\{\bar{a_{t}}\sim\pi_{\text{vla}}(\bar{a_{t}}\mid c_{t})\,\middle|\,\bar{a_{t}}\in\bar{E}^{k+1}\right\}.$$  |  | (7)
L112: 
L113: Finally, after the evolutionary search loop is completed, we select the highest-scoring elite action that best aligns with the steering objective for execution.
L114: cite52†Image: Refer to caption Fig. 4: Truncated Diffusion-Denoising Process. VLA-Pilot employs a truncated diffusion-denoising mechanism to mutate elite proposals, thereby enhancing action diversity and exploration capabilities to achieve better task alignment.
L115: ### III-D Iterative Steering Refinement
L116: We introduce an Iterative Steering Refinement mechanism that facilitates closed-loop correction of both the steering objective and the resulting actions. Concretely, we augment the original EPS-CoT reasoning module with a reflection step [cite53†23 ], in which the MLLM is prompted using four key components: the initial action proposal $a_{0}$, the executed action proposal $a_{t}^{\star}$, the post-execution task context $\bar{c}_{t}$, and the reasoning history $\mathcal{H}_{t}$ from the previous EPS-CoT step.
L117: Given this reflection-augmented input, the MLLM serves as a self-critic to refine the steering reward $R(a_{t}^{i};c_{t})$ and produce the steering success indicator $s$:
L118:  | $$s=\mathcal{F}_{\text{EPS-CoT}}(\Phi_{\text{MLLM}}(a_{0},a_{t}^{\star},\bar{c}_{t},\mathcal{H}_{t}))$$  |  | (8)
L119: If the MLLM detects inconsistencies in the inferred steering reward, a new reward is regenerated. Similarly, if the executed action is misaligned with the task context (i.e., $s=Flase$), VLA-Pilot continues the steering process until task completion. This closed-loop refinement ensures improvement in both the accuracy and contextual relevance of the steering process. The full workflow of our proposed VLA-Pilot is summarized in Algorithm cite54†1 .
L120: 
L121: Algorithm 1 VLA-Pilot
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SweeperBot: Making 3D Browsing Accessible through View Analysis and Visual Question Answering (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.14567v1)
citeturn6529view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({"ref_id":"turn6523view5","lineno":154}); Total lines: 532
L145: Stage 2: View Selection
L146: 
L147: While it is possible to prompt MLLM with all sampled views alongside the visual question, bad views can confuse MLLM during the answer generation process, causing hallucinations (Mo and Liu, cite83†2024 ). SweeperBot integrates a view selection pipeline (Figure cite111†2 b - e) to reject bad and irrelevant views. A good view is considered as both CLIP relevance and object relevance.
L148: 
L149: $\bullet$ Search CLIP-Relevant Views
L150: Just as sighted users gather information by exploring pertinent views of the target 3D model, the chosen views should be related to the visual questions. SweeperBot uses CLIP (OpenAI, cite112†2021 ; Radford et al., cite113†2021 ) to identify views that are semantically aligned with the visual question. This is realized by the similarity and the flatness score. A similar caveat in leveraging pre-trained CLIP model to retrieve text-conditioned views of 3D models was used in MemoVis (Chen et al., cite114†2024 ).
L151: Similarity ($s_{i}$). The similarity score is computed by the $cosine$ similarities between the CLIP-encoded sampled views and the comparative prompt, which is generated by joining key entities of the visual question. For example, the visual question “how many displays on the desk?” yields two entities: “display” and “desk”, leading to comparative prompt: “display, desk”. With all $s_{i}$, we use $z$-score filter to reject the views with $z_{\bm{s},i}=\frac{s_{i}-\mu_{\bm{s}}}{\sigma_{\bm{s}}}<-1$.
L152: Views with low similarities indicate low CLIP relevancy. With the running example and comparative prompt, SweeperBot will reject views with $s_{i}<\mu_{\bm{s}}-\sigma_{\bm{s}}=0.26$. Figure cite111†2 b shows the rejected views with $s_{i}$ being $0.24$ and $0.25$. These rejected views are rendered from the bottom of the desk that are less aligned with the comparative prompt.
L153: cite115†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3. Examples of using flatness score to measure the CLIP relevancy; (a) examples of how flatness score could be approximated; the flatness (b) and similarity score (c) at sampled rotational angles along latitudinal ($\alpha$) and longitudinal ($\beta$) axis, provided $r=0.5d+0.2$; (d - e) examples when flatness is used to enhance the reliability of the CLIP relevancy approximations.
L154: Flatness ($f_{i}$). Views with high $s_{i}$ may suggest strong CLIP relevance, but relying solely on $s_{i}$ can be unreliable. For example, while Figure cite116†3 e exhibits a slight higher $s_{i}$ than Figure cite116†3 f ($0.286$ vs. $0.281$), it would be easier to use Figure cite116†3 f for tallying the number of displays. Therefore, we define the flatness ($f_{i}$) score as the average $L1$-norm of the partial gradient of $s_{i}$.
L155: Intuitively, $f_{i}$ measures the difference of $s_{i}$ between the focused view and the neighboring views. Provided with a high similarity, the more consistent the score remains with minor adjustments in the viewing camera’s perspective, the greater the confidence in the CLIP relevance. For example, the flatness of the focus view in Figure cite116†3 a can be approximated by Equation cite117†1 .
L156: Figure cite116†3 b - c show $f_{i}$ and $s_{i}$ for all views, provided $r=0.5d+0.2$ (i.e., median distance between viewing camera and the 3D model). Figure cite116†3 d - f visualize the rendered views, with Figure cite116†3 f appearing to be the most suitable for counting the display with the lowest $f_{i}$. With $z$-score filter, we reject the views with $z_{\bm{f},i}=\frac{f_{i}-\mu_{\bm{f}}}{\sigma_{\bm{f}}}<-1$.
L157: (1)  |  | $$\begin{split}f_{focus}\approx\frac{1}{6}(|s_{focus}-s_{back}|+|s_{focus}-s_{front}|\\
L158: +|s_{focus}-s_{up}|+|s_{focus}-s_{down}|\\
L159: +|s_{focus}-s_{right}|+|s_{focus}-s_{left}|)\end{split}$$  |
L160: Finally, we used the image encoder of the CLIP (OpenAI, cite112†2021 ; Radford et al., cite113†2021 ) to remove the views that are well aligned (i.e.,  the repetitive views). Figure cite111†2 d shows an example in which only one view rendered from the front of the desk is retained. With the example of Figure cite111†2 , six CLIP-relevant views are retained.
L161: 
L162: $\bullet$ Search Object-Relevant Views
L163: While CLIP relevance narrows down the views, certain views like the bottom-up perspective of the desk model (last column, Figure cite111†2 d) may fail to address the visual question. SweeperBot uses the Grounding DINO (Liu et al., cite118†2023 ) to evaluate the object score ($o$), measured by the confidence of the existence of key objects. Incorporating Grounding DINO (Liu et al., cite118†2023 ) enables SweeperBot’s pipeline to more explicitly identify and analyze key objects within the sampled views.
L164: To evaluate $o_{i}$, SweeperBot computes the confidence of the bounding box using Grounding DINO (Liu et al., cite118†2023 ) by prompting each extracted entity. $o_{i}$ is then approximated by averaging all bounding box confidence. The inferred bounding boxes are omitted if they are perfectly overlapped with the entire 3D model. A zero-confidence box is added when object recognition fails. Figure cite111†2 e shows examples of how the “display” and “desk” are recognized.
L165: The two outliers being rendered from the bottom of the desk are rejected with a similar $z$-score filtering. Empirically, slightly higher thresholds than the defaults were chosen for box and text in Grounding DINO (Liu et al., cite118†2023 ) ($0.50$ and $0.35$).
L166: Stage 3: Answer Generation
L167: With the selected views, SweeperBot generates descriptions using both generative- and task-specific recognition-based models. We recognize the importance of providing reliable responses for confirmative and informative questions that are associated with factual details, such as the questions inquiring about counting specific objects, the details of the color, and the types of materials (DC2). SweeperBot first uses LLM to evaluate the type of visual question created.
L168: Upon counting-type questions (e.g., “how many displays are on the desk”), SweeperBot uses a compositional visual reasoning technique (Surís et al., cite119†2023 ) to generate the answer with the selected views. Empirical observation shows that employing compositional visual reasoning yields more accurate answers for counting-type questions with clear task goals. Without compositional visual reasoning, GPT-4V alone incorrectly considers “one display” for Figure cite105†11 b.
L169: We use MLLM to fulfill VQA for other types of questions like seeking descriptions of the design style of the desk, as generating accurate answers requires AI to grasp the contextual nuances of entire views, rather than merely focusing on specific components of interest.
L170: cite120†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4. Demonstration of compositional visual reasoning for answer generations in Stage 3. (a) Python code generated by an LLM for compositional visual reasoning; (b - e) recognition results of the “display” using Grounding DINO  (Liu et al., cite118†2023 ) by evaluating the selected views from Figure cite111†2 e; (f) synthesis generated answers from selected views.
L171: 
L172: $\bullet$ Compositional Visual Reasoning
L173: SweeperBot uses LLM to infer the procedural code for generating answers, followed by executing the AI-generated code with recognition-based models. The prompts used by ViperGPT (Surís et al., cite119†2023 ) were used for code generation, leveraging the LLM’s in-context learning capabilities. Figure cite121†4 a shows the generated Python code to count the number of displays.

